00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
first of all  our purpose is to strengthen the bonds  between the world's democracies i think  we are approaching a new world order  where you have an autocratic camp led by  china and the democratic camp led by the  united states as our aim is to make  the democratic camp as strong as  possible  but how can you do that when there are  as you say enormous countries like china  like russia that aren't following  democratic principles  well  in the run-up to this summit we have  conducted an opinion poll  which is actually the largest the  broadest opinion poll ever done on  democracy and 80 percent  of people all over the world want to  live in democracy  and furthermore  the free world represents  around 60 percent of the global economy  so if we stand together  if we  can unite  then we represent a formidable force  that will create some respect in beijing  and moscow  even though that is the case that that  it seems that most people want to live  in a democracy every year more elections  are being dismissed aren't they  in 2022 do you think democracy is is  under threat  absolutely for the 16th consecutive year  we have seen um  a global decline in freedom and  democracy  so  i think it's time it's high time to turn  the tide  we cannot take freedom and democracy for  granted we have to fight for it and we  see that currently in ukraine  the ukrainian people is actually  fighting on behalf of all of us  and we must stop putin  otherwise he will not stop he will  continue  to  conquer  peaceful neighbors if he isn't stuck in  ukraine  well on the subject of ukraine and  president putin you were of course uh  secretary general of nato so you  understand it workings very well  are you comfortable with how nato has  dealt with what has happened in ukraine  or do you think they should have done  more  well i think we have a duty  to help the ukrainians with everything  they need the ukrainian people has the  will to fight is our duty to provide  the means to fight  and so far i've been  satisfied with the unprecedented unity  within the nato alliance and among  democracies  in the whole world  when it comes to delivery of weapons but  i think we can do more i think the  european union should cut off all  financing of  putin's war machine it's a good step to  introduce an embargo of oil but that  should be followed by an embargo of gas  no oil no gas purchase in russia that  would bring  putin's war machine to its knees  has nato been emboldened do you think by  what putin has done of course you've  seen sweden and finland now talking  about membership  which they they had not done considered  for such a long time  yeah absolutely i mean  putin has achieved exactly the opposite  of what he wanted he has  uh against this sad backdrop of an  invasion in ukraine he has  created more unity within nato and now  he will also see nato being enlarged  with finland and sweden he gets  nato troops closer to  russian  borders so all in all  thanks to putin against this very sacked  sad backdrop  we have seen as strengthened nato  and in terms of countries doing more to  stop putin and nato doing water stop  putin in terms of embargoes do you think  the uk is doing enough is there anything  more you would like to see us doing  i think the uk has been at the very  forefront  of helping ukraine i also appreciate  the regular  updates on intelligence provided by the  uk  so i think  ukraine can really count the uk among  its closest friends and that may also be  necessary once the war is over  we have to ensure  that it will never happen again that  ukraine will be invaded so ukraine will  need security guarantees one way or the  other and i think the uk as well as the  u.s and other big nato allies will play  a crucial role in providing  a iron-clad security guarantees  to ukraine  how do you talk about the war being over  how do you think it will play out do you  see an end game  well i think the biggest risk right now  is a prolonged conflict  uh because putin and russia  are experts  in  prolonged simmering frozen conflicts for  decades they have uh occupied and eat  the eastern part of moldova since 2008  they have occupied applications south of  theater in the state of georgia since  2014 they have been in the crimea and  the eastern part of the runway region so  i think  putin's  goal might be  to continue to destabilize and weaken  ukraine through a prolonged  conflict i think this is currently the  biggest risk and that's why we have to  deliver all the weapons that ukraine  needs  we we have delivered some weapons  haven't we but also although we are not  delivering fighters um officially there  are some people who've gone in there  unofficially from the uk to fight and  two of those british soldiers we know  have been captured by by the russians  possibly now facing the death penalty is  there any hope for them that you can  offer to their families what what do you  think the international community could  do here  obviously we should do all we can to put  pressure on  russia but i'm not in a position to  to be too optimistic in that respect we  have seen how russia  violates  all international laws and  conventions  so i think the best  tool would be to continue  a maximum pressure uh on  russia the only language  that putin understands is a language of  strength  force and unity that's why we should  continue to put a maximum of pressure on  russia through delivery of all the  weapons  and cutting off all financing of  russia  but is there not also the danger that if  you do push back too hard um that he  then escalates it further  no on the contrary i think history has  taught us  that appeasement with dictators does not  lead to peace on the contrary it leads  to  war and  conflict  and i've noted that someone  has proposed that it  would be dangerous to humiliate  putin but i mean  setting the precedent  that an aggressor  can achieve their total gains  through  a peace deal after pursuing an  unjustified  war that will only encourage other  dictators as well it would set it would  send a very dangerous uh signal so i  think we have only one thing to do to  continue  helping the ukrainians to win this war

